{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QpaD6JKJXVvaBCiX6duXtU","type":"tool_result","content":"2:## *\"Everything that connects, and the traps that don't\"*\n8:## Attention Budget\n39:## 🧭 Soundings\n43:1. **Have you sat one full-length attempt under a clock — start to finish, no pauses, no looking anything up?** If yes: what was your score, and did you finish with time left or run out?\n45:2. **Which of the four domains is your weakest, and what specifically will you do about it this week?** Naming the domain is the easy half. The readiness signal is the plan.\n47:3. **Without looking anything up:** a PersistentVolumeClaim requests `ReadWriteOnce`. State in one sentence what that constrains, and what it does *not* constrain.\n49:4. **Name the recurring patterns this book has been building across all eighteen chapters.** Not the chapter titles. The patterns that showed up in four or five different chapters wearing different names. How many can you list from memory?\n51:5. **When you get a practice question wrong, what is your most common failure mode?** Misreading the stem? Coin-flipping between two survivors? Running out of clock and guessing? If you don't know, you have not been reviewing your wrong answers — only counting them.\n53:<details>\n56:**1.** No answer key — this is self-report. But note the trap: an *untimed* full-length attempt tells you almost nothing about pacing, and pacing is a distinct failure mode from knowledge. The Linux Foundation publishes 90 minutes for its multiple-choice exams [source: lf-mc-exam-faq-2026-08-31], and the timer cannot be paused, even through a connection fault [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31].\n58:**2.** No answer key. If you named a domain but not a plan, count this as half. §4 turns the plan half into something concrete.\n60:**3.** `ReadWriteOnce` constrains the volume to being mounted read-write by a **single node**, and multiple Pods on that same node can still use it. It does **not** mean one Pod; that is `ReadWriteOncePod`. And it does not enforce write protection at all. Access modes are node-count semantics, not permission semantics *[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 §4 — access modes and reclaim policies]*.\n62:**4.** No answer key here either. §1 supplies the list, and the point of asking first is that you try to generate it before you see it.\n64:**5.** No answer key. If you cannot name your failure mode, that is itself the finding.\n70:**5 of 5 — sit it.** You have timed data, a named weakness with a plan, cold retrieval on a discriminator, the thematic map, and self-knowledge about your errors. Use §5 and §6 as your schedule and stop adding material. Adding new sources at this stage costs you consolidation and buys you nothing.\n80:## Why This Chapter Matters\n92:## What You'll Learn\n107:## ☀️ §1 — Nine Threads Through Twenty Chapters\n166:> **★ Fixed Point**\n241:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: The Threads\n245:**Q1.** A cluster administrator installs a CustomResourceDefinition for a `BackupSchedule` resource. Users can create `BackupSchedule` objects successfully — `kubectl get backupschedules` returns them. No backups ever run. Name the pattern this is an instance of, and name two other places in this book where the same pattern appears. `[retrieval: ch3, ch6, ch10, ch13, ch17]`\n247:**Q2.** A `RoleBinding` in namespace `payments` references a `ClusterRole` named `secret-reader`. What permissions does the subject receive, and where? Derive your answer from a distinction established in Chapter 4 rather than recalling the matrix. `[retrieval: ch4, ch12]`\n249:**Q3.** A Pod is stuck in `Pending`. A colleague suggests checking `kubectl logs`. Explain why that is the wrong instrument, and name the cross-cutting thread that tells you which field to read instead. `[retrieval: ch5, ch7, ch13]`\n255:**Q1 — The absent-component pattern: an object without its component does nothing.**\n268:**Q2 — Permissions from the ClusterRole, but only inside `payments`.**\n276:**Q3 — Because `Pending` means no container has started, so there are no logs to read.**\n295:## 🟡 §2 — The Pairs That Cost Points\n427:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards**\n443:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: The Pairs\n447:**Q4.** A colleague says: \"Our Ingress is deprecated, so we need to migrate to Gateway API before our next upgrade or traffic will break.\" Identify every error in that sentence, and give the one-line discriminator for the pair being collapsed. `[retrieval: ch10]`\n449:**Q5.** You're handed a stem containing the word \"revision\" and four options. Two options describe Kubernetes objects, two describe Helm concepts. Before reading any option closely, what single question resolves which family the stem belongs to? Then state the same for \"rollback.\" `[retrieval: ch6, ch14]`\n455:**Q4 — Two errors. Ingress is frozen, not deprecated; and nothing breaks on upgrade.**\n465:**Q5 — For \"revision\": *whose* history? For \"rollback\": *whose* history is being rewound?**\n487:## ⚪ §3 — Ninety Minutes\n497:> **★ Fixed Point**\n559:## ⚪ §4 — Where the Weight Actually Is\n598:1. **Your worst-scoring chapter in Domain 1.** It is 44% of the exam. A weak chapter there costs more than a weak chapter anywhere else, arithmetically.\n599:2. **Community and Collaboration**, if you have not deliberately studied it. Highest return per hour in the book.\n600:3. **§2 of this chapter**, worked actively rather than read. Discrimination failures are cheap to fix and expensive to leave.\n601:4. **Your worst-scoring chapter in Domain 2.** 28% and the second-largest surface.\n602:5. **A timed full-length attempt**, if you have not sat one. Pacing is a distinct skill and it is invisible until you meet the clock.\n608:## ⚪ §5 — Using The Lodestar\n646:## ⚪ §6 — The Week Before\n698:## Exam Alert! 🚨\n704:1. **The absent-component pattern, in its exact form:** *an object without its component does nothing.* It converts at least five separate gotchas — Ingress, NetworkPolicy, CSI, metrics-server, VPA — into one rule you apply on sight.\n705:2. **The RBAC four-way binding matrix, derived rather than memorized.** The binding's scope sets the grant's scope. Everything else falls out of Chapter 4's namespaced/cluster-scoped distinction.\n706:3. **Additive, allow-only, no deny.** One semantic shared by RBAC and NetworkPolicy, built independently by two different groups.\n707:4. **The version-skew numbers:** kubelet up to three minor versions back and never newer, kubectl within one either way, three supported minor releases [source: k8s-version-skew-policy-2026-08-31].\n708:5. **Community and Collaboration vocabulary.** Highest ratio of exam presence to study time in the book, and the competency most likely to have been skipped.\n719:## Practice Questions\n725:**1.** A cluster has a `NetworkPolicy` in namespace `api` selecting Pods labeled `tier=backend` and allowing ingress from Pods labeled `tier=frontend`. A frontend Pod in the same namespace cannot reach a backend Pod. No other NetworkPolicy exists anywhere. What is the most likely cause?\n734:**2.** A Pod has been in `Pending` for ten minutes. Which sequence of diagnostic steps is correct?\n743:**3.** `kube-apiserver` is at 1.37. Which combination is supported?\n752:**4.** A `RoleBinding` in namespace `web` grants a `ClusterRole` called `pod-reader` to a ServiceAccount. A second `ClusterRoleBinding` grants the same `ClusterRole` to the same ServiceAccount. What can the ServiceAccount read?\n761:**5.** An application deployed via Helm has been upgraded three times. A bad configuration shipped in the third upgrade. The team wants the previous state of *all* objects the chart manages. Which is correct?\n770:**6.** A PersistentVolume with `persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain` had its bound PVC deleted. A new PVC with identical requirements is created and stays `Pending`. Why?\n779:**7.** A team runs an Argo CD instance that watches a Git repository and applies manifests to the cluster. Which statement correctly describes the security property this delivery model provides?\n788:**8.** A Deployment's Pods show phase `Running` and a readiness probe that is failing. What is the observable consequence?\n797:**9.** An organization wants to define who is responsible for approving a new project into the CNCF, and separately who decides the foundation's budget. Which pairing is correct?\n806:**10.** A team wants a Pod's CPU and memory *requests* adjusted automatically based on observed usage, without adding replicas. They install nothing extra and create a `VerticalPodAutoscaler` manifest — the API rejects it as an unknown kind. Why, and what pattern is this?\n817:**1 — B. The CNI plugin does not implement NetworkPolicy.**\n825:**2 — B. Describe, read events and the scheduler's message, check capacity against requests.**\n833:**3 — C. `kubelet` 1.34, `kubectl` 1.38.**\n841:**4 — B. Pods cluster-wide.**\n849:**5 — B. `helm rollback <release> 2`.**\n857:**6 — B. The PV is `Released`, not `Available`.**\n865:**7 — B. Credentials live inside the cluster; the agent reaches out.**\n873:**8 — B. Removed from Service endpoints, no restart.**\n881:**9 — A. The TOC approves projects; the Governing Board decides the budget.**\n889:**10 — B. VPA is an add-on; this is the absent-component pattern.**\n901:## Chapter Summary\n920:## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n930:## The Voyage Ahead"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"162e0c3b-d249-45ca-887d-880c72b06ba7","uuid":"c3d470fc-893d-409b-bcbc-efe41979cfa1","timestamp":"2026-08-31T22:58:33.351Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"2:## *\"Everything that connects, and the traps that don't\"*\n8:## Attention Budget\n39:## 🧭 Soundings\n43:1. **Have you sat one full-length attempt under a clock — start to finish, no pauses, no looking anything up?** If yes: what was your score, and did you finish with time left or run out?\n45:2. **Which of the four domains is your weakest, and what spe