{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VyuorwnfrQcr1K17qtdiBF","type":"tool_result","content":"276\t**Q3 — Because `Pending` means no container has started, so there are no logs to read.**\n277\t\n278\tA Pod in `Pending` has been accepted by the API server but has not been scheduled onto a node, or has been scheduled and its images have not been pulled. In the unscheduled case no container exists anywhere. `kubectl logs` asks the kubelet on the Pod's node for container output; there is no node and no container.\n279\t\n280\tThe thread is **Pod phase versus container state** (Ch 5 §5). The phase tells you where in the lifecycle the Pod is, and the phase determines which instrument is even applicable. Read the phase first, then `kubectl describe` for events and conditions. For a `Pending` Pod the scheduler's message about why no node was feasible is the actual diagnostic *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §2 — diagnosing a Pod that will not start]*.\n281\t\n282\tIf you said \"logs would be empty,\" you are right but incomplete. The useful answer names what to read *instead*, and why the phase told you.\n283\t\n284\t---\n285\t\n286\t**Checkpoint: You've Now Confirmed**\n287\t✓ You can recognize the absent-component pattern from a symptom description\n288\t✓ You can derive the RBAC binding matrix rather than recall it\n289\t✓ You read the phase before you reach for an instrument\n290\t\n291\tNow the harder half. §2 is where the points are."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"162e0c3b-d249-45ca-887d-880c72b06ba7","uuid":"951bd600-91d7-429a-9ab4-659c5496dec0","timestamp":"2026-08-31T22:58:41.480Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-19/draft-v1.md","content":"**Q3 — Because `Pending` means no container has started, so there are no logs to read.**\n\nA Pod in `Pending` has been accepted by the API server but has not been scheduled onto a node, or has been scheduled and its images have not been pulled. In the unscheduled case no container exists anywhere. `kubectl logs` asks the kubelet on the Pod's node for container output; there is no node and no container.\n\nThe thread is **Pod phase versus container state** (Ch 5 §5). The phase tells you where in the lifecycle the Pod is, and the phase determines which instrument is even applicable. Read the phase first, then `kubectl describe` for events and conditions. For a `Pending` Pod the scheduler's message about why no node was feasible is the actual diagnostic *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §2 — diagnosing a Pod that will not start]*.\n\nIf you said \"logs would be empty,\" you are right but incomplete. The useful answer names what to read *instead*, and why the phase told you.\n\n---\n\n**Checkpoint: You've Now Confirmed**\n✓ You can recognize the absent-component pattern from a symptom description\n✓ You can derive the RBAC binding matrix rather than recall it\n✓ You read the phase before you reach for an instrument\n\nNow the harder half. §2 is where the points are.","numLines":16,"startLine":276,"totalLines":940}}}
